From ea8058816b11af48b28519078dbac44074d00ed8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Derek Ross <derekross@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 16 Oct 2026 12:04:51 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 52/52] ipa: simple: Add the Look algorithm and tune it on the
 Pixel 7 Pro

Drive the debayer's hue, vibrance and tone curve from a new Look
algorithm: the tuning file gives the 12 hue sectors' shifts in degrees
and vibrances, the chroma at which vibrance falls to none, and the
shadow lift, which the AGC's output prediction follows.

Tune it for the Google Pixel 7 Pro's four cameras on our own daylight
shots, compared with our own shots of the same scenes from another
phone. Measured there, greens came out 15-45 degrees (HSV) on the cyan
side of the reference and with half its saturation, and blue skies on
the violet side; reds were close. So: greens 25 degrees towards
yellow, blue skies 10 out of violet, vibrance 1.0 for greens, 0.8 for
blues and 0.2 for reds, and a lift of 0.45. Replayed offline on nine
pairs of shots, the mean output moved from 32 sRGB levels under the
reference to within one (median within 7, 95th percentile 12 over), the
sky's hue from 15 to 10 degrees off, and the greens' saturation to the
reference's; the greens' HSV hue moved little (15 to 13 degrees off),
so the green shift may want more in daylight.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
